THE BROKEN CROWN
Letters of Sergeant Elin Marsh, River Watch

To my sister Anna, in the hills, where I hope this finds you and hope harder it finds you far from any road.

The king is dead. Not in battle, not in bed — on the stairs of his own chapel, and the only man who saw it happen has since been hanged for treason, which tells you everything about who is writing the laws now.

Duke Maren Vale wore mourning grey to the funeral and a crown to the feast after it. The heralds call him Lord Protector. The soldiers he marched into the capital call him nothing at all, because his soldiers do not talk, not even to each other. Winter Duke, the market women say, and spit.

Half the army knelt to him. I want to write that they were cowards, but the truth is worse: they were tired, and he paid on time.

The other half is us. The River Watch, what's left of the Home Companies, and every knight too stubborn or too honest to kneel. Ser Aldric Dane rode into our camp three nights ago with the old king's standard folded under his arm like firewood. He is not a talkative man. He put it on the table and said, "Well," and that was the whole speech.

Wren says the Duke's men are already on the Marrow road, coming to take the tollgate bridge and with it every farm between here and the Greywater. Wren is our scout and is never wrong, which makes her tiresome at cards and indispensable in a war.

So it starts at a tollgate. Not a fortress, not a field of banners. A bridge with a gate on it, where the toll used to be two coppers.

Tomorrow the toll goes up.

— Elin
